{"body": {"notes": "integrate your shadow", "title": "Penumbra", "artist": "Saka", "artwork": {"nft": null, "info": {"uri": "https://ipfs.io/ipfs/bafybeigsy7lgtpbli6ygsztwlyc2fqzcpd52r5ifhj77rfftkfgusayqga", "mimeType": "image/png"}, "isNft": false}, "project": {"notes": "Hong Kong-based electronic artist Saka has signed to Dome of Doom and will release the Penumbra EP across digital platforms June 4th. Penumbra is an introspective work that centers around the emotional vortex of a past relationship with each track serving as a chronological representation of each phase. “To be more on the nose, in order, "Lilac" represents the sort of initial honeymoon phase,” Saka adds. “"Prescient" represents starting to realize that things might not be so perfect after all. "Penumbra" exemplifies right before the breakup, and "Let me Live" being right after.” Saka's new EP differs in sound design and structural approach to past works, rooted in the language of songwriter-driven electronic music and accentuated with a sense of unbridled experimentation.


On Penumbra, Saka presents his vocals on record for the first time with “Prescient,” also introducing a new collaborator to his catalog with the last track, “Let Me Live (feat. Amethyst)." Both pieces are soaked in a wave of deep emotional resonance, supplanted with harmonious lines, amorphous overtones, and complex patterns. Panoramic and tranquil, it sets a unique sense of balance to their counterpart bookends: the dance-floor euphoria of the EPs introductory piece “Lilac,” and the refractacted neck-snapping wild sonics of the title-track. All sides serving as important energetic windows into the type of emotional roller coaster Saka was on during his past relationship. The Penumbra recording sessions began in May of 2020 and the mixing/mastering process was finalized this year by Saka. 


A lot of the sound design for Penumbra was created on Ableton, mostly centered around the use of the Novation Peak, a synth Saka purchased at the beginning of 2020. Operator, Ableton's native FM synth, was also heavily used in the recording process. Saka adds further insight into the writing and recording behind Penumbra: “I began writing around the concept of Penumbra at the end of May last year. It's actually mostly about an ex, and the feelings I experienced throughout our relationship. Each track sort of represents a different time period of the relationship, in chronological order. I didn't really try to make any of these musical ideas fit into any specific sort of overarching genre, sort of just wrote from the heart in the sonic lexicon that I have. I wrote largely without intention, wanted to get my feelings out in musical format, so the tunes range from beats to DnB to garage. I was pretty patient with myself, I didn't really write this EP with any sort of deadline for myself. In fact, I spent time throughout 2020 largely creatively wandering, trying to figure out where I belonged in this electronic music space I'm in, all the while writing ideas for this throughout the year. I think Penumbra is sort of the culmination of me not really giving that attention anymore. It's really just music I want to hear now that represents how I felt emotionally at the time.”", "title": "Penumbra", "artwork": {"nft": null, "info": {"uri": "https://ipfs.io/ipfs/bafybeigsy7lgtpbli6ygsztwlyc2fqzcpd52r5ifhj77rfftkfgusayqga", "mimeType": "image/png"}, "isNft": false}}, "version": "catalog-20210202", "duration": 253.32, "mimeType": "audio/wav", "visualizer": null, "trackNumber": 3}, "origin": {"encoding": "rlp", "algorithm": "secp256k1", "publicKey": "0xc236541380fc0C2C05c2F2c6c52a21ED57c37952", "signature": "0x57b3ff6fba89256fd6ea50b100008c6a16a435ce56dcb65e279113177b79e42e76ebcb5a19f5d886f30176c2fb204486fc99a97f63f53c8c1f53976d9eaf33a71c"}}

