{"name": "مذاهب النسيان / The Tenets of Forgetting - B1 - مذاهب النسيان : The Tenets of Forgetting", "image": "ar://lUwx6HyaAG8T3JaHaQ1If9rektBXIDW9XBtRGgJvw64", "title": "B1 - مذاهب النسيان : The Tenets of Forgetting", "artist": "Éditions Appærent", "artwork": {"uri": "ar://lUwx6HyaAG8T3JaHaQ1If9rektBXIDW9XBtRGgJvw64", "mimeType": "image/jpeg"}, "version": "catalog-20220222", "duration": 290.377, "mimeType": "audio/x-wav", "description": "The Tenets of Forgetting is the long-awaited follow-up to MSYLMA’s lauded 2019 debut, Dhil-un Taht Shajarat Al-Zaqum. This time he’s joined by fellow musician and producer ISMAEL for seven tracks, built around a lush palette of synths and MSYLMA’s singular voice.

Written and produced in Cairo between 2015 and 2020, The Tenets of Forgetting is the first collaboration between MSYLMA and ISMAEL. Like his first record, The Tenets of Forgetting finds MSYLMA singing in classical Arabic, and the musicians have enlisted Nariman Youssef to provide translations of the lyrics. This gesture of openness runs parallel to the themes of the record; romance and vulnerability; growth and change; pathos and passion. Listeners who aren’t familiar with pre-Islamic poetry or classical Arabic will find the lyrics just as rich and beautiful as the vocal melodies imply, telling a story of love, loss, self-doubt, and grave introspection.

Musically, the album moves in broad, painterly sweeps of 808 kicks, rich synthesizer chords, and MYSLMA’s soaring, plaintive voice. The English translations reveal a second world of mathematically sound rhythmic structures and poetry in the lyrics. The music pushes and pulls in both tempo and timbre, swirling both around and underneath MSYLMA’s voice but never losing a sense of self-contained narrative.", "trackNumber": 5, "external_url": "https://catalog.works/b1-the-tenets-of-forgetting", "animation_url": "ar://OB13lQhdgbmLYbgjX2G4Ckzpd2Zftix8QdOZyL2sMUI", "losslessAudio": "ar://X9lvdN8MSGCxptoC2bVg4wnWPrzmTG8h_oB7L6MsUUU"}

{"name": "The Man Who Shot th Sheriff - HeartWerk - Plans That Never Pan Out - Low Recordings", "image": "ar://0R2C8R0KWW8pEXj_fqEw2efrQCufhH5HscQIh6MzVbk", "title": "HeartWerk - Plans That Never Pan Out - Low Recordings", "artist": "Low Recordings", "artwork": {"uri": "ar://0R2C8R0KWW8pEXj_fqEw2efrQCufhH5HscQIh6MzVbk", "mimeType": "image/jpeg"}, "version": "catalog-20220222", "duration": 344.059, "mimeType": "audio/wav", "description": "Tulsa resident HeartWerk is hard at work in the state of Oklahoma spreading the soulful sounds of authentic house & techno to the masses. His excellent U Don’t Dance label which he uses as a platform for his music, in addition to others, has carved out a tight-knit community in Tulsa’s local electronic music scene and continues to do so with every release and party. HeartWerk now returns to the dynamic, LA-based collective and label, Low Recordings following their exquisite compilation that picked up an array of support from James Zabiela, Gene Farris, Nick Holder, Photonz, and many more.

‘There’s A New Cowboy In Town’ kicks off with sultry pads, fluttering melodies, and irresistible subs that draw you in instantly before ‘Plans That Never Pan Out’ lays down vibrant rhythms fused delicately with soul-infused leads and graceful bass that reverberates underneath. Up next, ‘Oklahoma Corral’ is a dreamy cut combining sparkling synths, infectious grooves, and divine atmospheres whilst ‘Printing Clouds Above Us’ displays organic field recordings that harmonies with soothing keys, and celestial ambiance. Rounding off the EP with pure energy is ‘Still In Love’, which focuses on effervescent, shuffling drums, resounding bass stabs, and rolling vocal samples.
